    {
      "id": "liaisons-enchainements",
      "title": "Les liaisons, les enchaînements et l'élision",
      "minutes": 6,
      "lede": "In French, words are not separated by pauses: sounds flow from one word to the next. This is called continuity.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "title": "Trois phénomènes",
          "headers": [
            "Phénomène",
            "Exemple",
            "Ce qu'on entend"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "élision",
              "j'ai mal à la gorge",
              "le e de je disparaît : j'ai"
            ],
            [
              "enchaînement",
              "une infirmière",
              "u-nin-fir-mière : le n de « une » s'attache à la voyelle suivante"
            ],
            [
              "liaison",
              "vous avez des antibiotiques",
              "vou-za-vé dé-zan-ti-bio-tiques"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "tone": "warning",
          "title": "Gotchas",
          "text": "- Enchaînement: a pronounced final consonant joins the next word beginning with a vowel: elle a mal → e-la-mal. Il a mal à l'oreille → i-la-ma-la-lo-reille.\n- Liaison: a normally silent final consonant is pronounced before a vowel: vous_avez (z), ils_ont (z), des_amis (z), un_ami (n), les_enfants (z). Final -s, -x, -z sound like z; -d like t; -n like n.\n- Liaison is always made between determiner and noun (les_amis, un_ami), pronoun and verb (vous_avez, ils_ont, on_a). It is never made after et (un garçon et un homme: no liaison) and never before a so-called aspirated h (les héros: no liaison, but les_hommes).\n- Elision: je, me, te, se, le, la, ne, de, que, si (before il) drop their vowel before a vowel or mute h: j'ai, l'infirmier, qu'il, s'il.\n- These phenomena make French sound continuous. Practice by saying the group as one long word: j'ai-mal-à-la-gorge."
        },
        {
